Mandatory DRM CSC stages: runtime-selectable PQ domain convention

The NVIDIA Full pipeline's two non-bypassable 1D curve stages (CSC0LUT
PQ-125-inverse-EOTF and CSC1LUT PQ-125-EOTF) are fixed functions: the
nvidia-drm commit path never reads their colorop state, so nothing KWin
programs changes what they apply. The driver bridges only the bypassable
DEGAMMA named curve to the DRM UAPI convention (10,000 nits -> 125.0)
via an implicit degamma multiplier; its internal transfer-function
convention maps 10,000 nits -> 1.0.

Patch 0016's compensation assumes the UAPI convention for these
mandatory stages. If the hardware instead applies the internal
convention, the composed transform is wrong by a full PQ-domain factor.
An offline harness against the probed NVIDIA Full graph measures:

  assumption matches hardware: worst error <= 0.6 nits of 420 (LUT
  quantization level), zero domain clamps
  assumption mismatches: mean linear error 73-95 nits, ~322k domain
  clamp events per grid

This patch makes the mandatory-stage scale runtime-selectable via
KWIN_DRM_NVIDIA_CSC_PQ_SCALE (values "1" or "125"). The default stays
125 (the UAPI assumption), so stock behavior is unchanged and a wrong
guess cannot silently pass; it can only be resolved by the controlled
live test in docs/DIRECT_SCANOUT_NVIDIA_INVESTIGATION_2026-08-23.md.
Bypassable named curves always use the UAPI scale because the driver
bridges them explicitly.

Behavior remains behind the exact-kernel gate and the color-pipeline
opt-in from patches 0013 and later.

